What is the significance of axillary lymph node status in primary breast cancer?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the significance of axillary lymph node status in primary breast cancer?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The status of axillary lymph nodes is crucial in determining the course of early-stage breast cancer treatment. Axillary lymph nodes are small, oval-shaped organs located in the upper legs, where cancer cells can spread through the lymphatic system. The axillary lymph nodes are particularly important because cancer cells from the breast often spread to these nodes first.

Axillary lymph node status refers to whether or not cancer cells are found in the axillary lymph nodes. This is determined by a tumor biopsy, either during the initial breast cancer diagnosis or during a separate procedure called a sentinel lymph node biopsy.

Axenatomic lymph node status is important because it helps healthcare providers determine the stage of the cancer, which in turn influences the type and extent of treatment needed. Axenatomic lymph node status is also associated with the likelihood of cancer recurrence and survival.

In general, if no cancer cells are found in the axillary lymph nodes (no-node or low-node status), the cancer is less aggressive and the risk of recurrence is lower. This may mean that less aggressive treatment options, such as hormone therapy or radiation therapy, may be appropriate.

If cancer cells are found in the axillary lymph nodes (positive node status), the cancer is more aggressive and the risk of recurrence is higher. This may mean that more aggressive treatment options, such as advanced radiation therapy, chemotherapy, or targeted therapy, may be needed.

In summary, the status of axillary lymph nodes is crucial in determining the stage of breast cancer and the appropriate treatment plan. It is an important factor in predicting the likelihood of cancer recurrence and survival.
