The definition of **whole-body oxygen delivery** is the amount of oxygen delivered to the tissues of the body per unit time. It is a measure of the body's ability to supply oxygen to its tissues for energy production and other metabolic processes.

Whole-body oxygen delivery is calculated using the following formula:

\[
\text{VO}_2 = \text{VO}_T \times \text{VO}_2/\text{VO}_T
\]

Where:
- **VO₂** is the **whole-body oxygen consumption** (in mL/min or L/min),
- **VO_T** is the **total volume of oxygen in the tissues** (in mL or L),
- **VO₂/VO_T** is the **oxygen extraction fraction** (a fraction representing the proportion of oxygen extracted from the blood by the tissues).

This formula is derived from Fick's equation, which relates the rate of gas transfer (in this case, oxygen) to the surface area, the rate of blood flow, and the difference in partial pressures of the gas between the tissues and the blood.

Alternatively, whole-body oxygen delivery can be calculated using the following formula:

\[
\text{VO}_2 = \text{VO}_T \times \text{VO}_2/\text{VO}_T
