</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
In the **GELA LNH-93-1** study, 647 patients ≤60 years old with low-risk (IPI 0), stages I–II intermediate-risk NHL (including extranodal or bulky disease) were randomized to aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation) or standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT). The aggressive chemotherapy arm (ACVBP) showed superior 5-year event-free survival (82% vs. 74%) and overall survival (90% vs. 81%) compared to CHOP + RT, regardless of bulky disease. However, ACVBP was associated with high toxicity (e.g., secondary malignancies), limiting its clinical use.  

$\boxed{\text{ACVBP improved EFS and OS but was too toxic for routine use.}}$
</GROUND_TRUTH>

