","The metal ion in the active site of the metalloenzyme carbonic anhydrase is zinc (Zn²⁺). The coordination number of the zinc ion in the active site is 4. In carbonic anhydrase, the zinc ion is coordinated to three histidine residues and a water molecule or hydroxide ion.

The coordination number of the zinc ion plays a crucial role in the catalytic activity of the enzyme. The zinc ion acts as a Lewis acid, polarizing the bound water molecule or hydroxide ion, making it more nucleophilic. This facilitates the nucleophilic attack of the water molecule on the carbon dioxide substrate, leading to the formation of bicarbonate.

The coordination number of 4 allows the zinc ion to provide the optimal balance between Lewis acid strength and structural stability. A higher coordination number would result in a less acidic metal center, while a lower coordination number would lead to a less stable enzyme structure. The tetrahedral geometry of the zinc ion in the active site also allows for optimal positioning of the substrate and the nucleophile, further enhancing the catalytic activity of the enzyme.
