","The optimal ratio of titanium dioxide (TiO2) nanoparticles and graphene oxide (GO) in a composite material for use in high-performance dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs) depends on several factors, including the specific types of TiO2 and GO used, the synthesis method, and the desired properties of the final material. However, in general, a lower ratio of GO to TiO2 is more recommended. This is because while GO can enhance the performance of DSSCs, adding too much can lead to a decrease in the overall efficiency.

The optimal ratio of TiO2 to GO nanoparticles in a composite material for use in high-performance DSSCs is typically in the range of 80:20 to 60:40, with the TiO2 content being the majority. This ratio can be adjusted depending on the specific application and desired properties of the material.

The ratio of TiO2 to GO affects the overall efficiency and stability of the solar cell in the following ways:

1. Light absorption: TiO2 is the primary light-absorbing material in DSSCs, and its high bandgap allows it to absorb light in the visible and ultraviolet regions. The addition of GO can enhance the light absorption of the composite material by increasing the light scattering and reflection properties of the material. However, if the ratio of GO to TiO2 is too high, the light absorption of the composite material may decrease due to the increased scattering and reflection of light by the GO.

