"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. The σ orbital is formed by head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a symmetrical electron density distribution.  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. The π orbital is formed by side-by-side overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a directional electron density distribution.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-on overlap (e.g., sp-s, sp²-sp², sp²-sp²) and are more stable than π orbitals, while π orbitals are formed by side-by-side overlap (e.g., sp²-sp²) and are less stable.  

$\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density along the axis; π orbital: electron density above/below the axis}}$
