","To selectively reduce the aldehyde group in a molecule containing an aldehyde, ester, and chloromethyl group, such as the one described in Scheme 2.73, you can use a reducing agent that selectively reacts with aldehydes without affecting the other functional groups. One such reagent is sodium cyanoborohydride (NaBH₃CN).

Sodium cyanoborohydride is a milder reducing agent compared to other reducing agents like sodium borohydride (NaBH₄) or lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH₄). This means it selectively reduces aldehydes to primary alcohols without reducing the ester or chloromethyl group.

The reaction mechanism involves the formation of an intermediate borbyal complex, which is then hydrolyzed to yield the primary alcohol. The ester and chloromethyl group remain unaffected in this process.

In summary, to selectively reduce the aldehyde group in the given molecule, use sodium cyanoborohydride (NaBH₃CN) as the reducing agent. This reagent selectively reduces aldehydes without affecting the ester or chloromethyl group, allowing for the selective transformation of the aldehyde functionality.
