","The pH of a solution has a significant effect on the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles. Electrokinetic potential is the potential difference between the dispersion medium and the surface of a charged particle in a colloidal system. It is influenced by the surface charge of the particle, which is in turn affected by the pH of the solution.

At low pH values (acidic conditions), the surface charge of colloidal particles is generally positive due to the protonation of functional groups on the particle surface. As the pH increases (alkaline conditions), the protonation of these functional groups decreases, leading to a decrease in the positive surface charge. At a certain pH value, known as the isoelectric point (IEP), the net surface charge of the particle is zero. Beyond the IEP, the surface charge becomes negative due to the deprotonation of functional groups.

The relationship between pH and electrokinetic potential can be described by the following equation:

Zeta potential (ζ) = -ve × (pH - IEP)

where:
- ζ is the electrokinetic potential,
- ve is a constant that depends on the specific particle and electrolyte,
- pH is the pH of the solution,
- IEP is the isoelectric point of the particle.

In a solution of known ionic strength, the electrokinetic potential of colloidal particles will be influenced by the pH of the solution. At low pH values, the electrokinetic potential will be positive, and as the pH increases, the electrokinetic potential will decrease and eventually become negative. The isoelectric point (IEP) will be the pH value at which the electrokinetic potential is zero.

It is important to note that the relationship between pH and electrokinetic potential can be affected by the presence of other ions in the solution, as well as the specific surface chemistry of the colloidal particles.
