","To calculate the polarization in the electrochemical cell, we first need to determine the cell potential (E_cell) under non-standard conditions. The cell potential can be calculated using the Nernst equation:

E_cell = E°_cell - (RT/nF) * ln(Q)

where E°_cell is the standard cell potential, R is the gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K), T is the temperature in Kelvin (assuming standard conditions, T = 298 K), n is the number of electrons transferred in the redox reaction, F is the Faraday constant (96485 C/mol), and Q is the reaction quotient.

First, we need to determine the standard cell potential (E°_cell) by finding the difference between the standard reduction potentials of the two half-reactions:

E°_cell = E°_cathode - E°_anode
E°_cell = E°_Ag+ + E°_Cu2+
E°_cell = (+0.7996 V) - (+0.342 V)
E°_cell = +0.4476 V

Next, we need to write the balanced redox reaction and determine the reaction quotient (Q). The balanced redox reaction is:

Ag+ + Cu → Ag + Cu2+

The reaction quotient (Q) can be calculated using the concentrations of the species involved:

Q = [Cu2+]/[Ag+]
Q = (0.2 M)/(0.1 M)
Q = 2

Now we can plug the values into the Nernst equation:

E_cell = E°_cell - (RT/nF) * ln(Q)
E_cell = 0.4476 V - (8.314 J/mol·K * 298 K / (2 * 96485 C/mol)) * ln(2)
E_cell ≈ 0.4476 V - 0.0296 V
E_cell ≈ 0.418 V

The polarization in the electrochemical cell is the difference between the open-circuit potential (E_cell) and the actual potential (E_actual) at the electrode. In this case, we are assuming negligible resistance in the cell, so the actual potential (E_actual) is the same as the open-circuit potential (E_cell). Therefore, the polarization in this electrochemical cell is negligible.
