Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis where the reaction produces a product with a specific stereochemistry (e.g., R or S configuration) with 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means that only one enantiomer is formed, and no the other enantiomer is produced. This is distinct from other types of asymmetric synthesis, where the reaction produces a mixture of enantiomers, often with high selectivity but not complete removal of one enantiomer.  

For example, in a reaction where the product has two stereocenters, an asymmetric synthesis could produce a 90% ee of one enantiomer and 10% of the other. This is still high selectivity, but it is not absolute asymmetric synthesis.  

In contrast, in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the reaction would produce only the desired enantiomer, with no of the other enantiomer present.  

**Example:**  
A classic example of asymmetric synthesis is the hydrogenation of methyl vinyl ketone (MVK) to produce 1-butanol. This reaction can be catalyzed by chiral rhodium complexes, such as the rhodium-BINAP complex.  

- In a **classical asymmetric hydrogenation** (e.g., using a chiral rhodium-BINAP complex), the reaction can achieve high enantioselectivity, producing 90% or more of one enantiomer.  
- In **absolute asymmetric synthesis**, the reaction would produce only one enantiomer of 1-butanol, with no of the other enantiomer.  

This could be achieved by using a chiral catalyst that reacts only with one enantiomer of MVK, or by using a chiral auxiliary that selectively reacts with one enantiomer of MVK.  

Thus, the key difference between asymmetric synthesis and absolute asymmetric synthesis is the level of enantiomeric excess achieved.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Asymmetric synthesis: } \geq 90\% \text{ ee (e.g., 90\% one enantiomer, 10\% other enantiomer)} \\
&\text{Absolute asymmetric synthesis: } 100\% \text{ ee (only one enantiomer produced)}
\end{aligned}
}

