","To prove the non-commutativity of the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \), we analyze the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \). The expressions for these derivatives are as follows:

\[
aD_t^p f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t t^{p-1} \frac{d}{dt} \left[ \int_0^t (t - s)^{p-1} f(s) \, ds \right] \, dt,
\]

\[
aD_t^q f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t t^{q-1} \frac{d}{dt} \left[ \int_0^t (t - s)^{q-1} f(s) \, ds \right] \, dt.
\]

Substituting these into the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) and simplifying yields a double integral with non-trivial terms involving \( f(s) \). In general, this double integral cannot be simplified to zero, indicating that \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute.

However, if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions vanish, the difference simplifies to zero. The lower terminal conditions are \( f(0) = 0 \) and \( f'(0) = 0 \). These conditions ensure that the integrals vanish, making \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) commute. The exact conditions are:

\[
f(0) = 0, \quad f'(0) = 0, \quad f''(0) = 0, \quad \text{and} \quad f'''(0) = 0,
\]

where the number of derivatives \( f^{(k)}(0) = 0 \) must be at least \( p + q - 1 \). This ensures that all terms involving the lower terminal conditions vanish in the difference \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) - aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \). 

Thus, the commutativity of \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) depends on the specific function \( f(t) \) and the values of \( p \) and \( q \). 

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{No commutativity if } f(0) \neq 0, f'(0) \neq 0, f''(0) \neq 0, \text{ or } f'''(0) \neq 0, \\
&\text{Commutativity if } f(0) = 0, f'(0) = 0, f''(0) = 0, \text{ and } f^{(k)}(0) = 0 \text{ for } k \geq 3 \text{ and } k \leq p + j - 1.
\end{aligned}
}
