A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Convert the speed of the train to meters per second**:  
   \( 45 \, \text{km/hr} \times \frac{1000 \, \text{m}}{1 \, \text{km}} \times \frac{1 \, \text{hr}}{3600 \, \text{s}} = 12 \, \text{m/s} \).

2. **Calculate the relative speed of the train relative to the jogger**:  
   \( 12 \, \text{m/s} - 9 \, \text{m/s} = 3 \, \text{m/s} \).  
   (Since the train and jogger are moving in the same direction, subtract their speeds.)

3. **Calculate the length of the train**:  
   The train passes the jogger in 25 seconds, so its length is the product of its relative speed and the time taken:  
   \( 3 \, \text{m/s} \times 25 \, \text{s} = 75 \, \text{m} \).

4. **Final Answer**:  
   The train is \(\boxed{75 \, \text{m}}\) long.
