","To prove this, we use the definition of a polyhedral cone and the properties of linear spaces.

1. **Existence of the Hyperplane**:
   - The cone \( C \) is spanned by the vectors \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n \).
   - The set \( \{a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n\} \) is a basis of \( C \), and its span is the entire cone.
   - The set \( \text{span}(a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n) \) is a Euclidean plane in \( \mathbb{R}^n \).
   - The hyperplane \( H \) can be defined as the hyperplane orthogonal to the span of \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n \) and passing through the origin.

2. **Properties of \( H \) and \( b \)**:
   - Since \( b \notin C \), \( b \) cannot be expressed as a linear combination of \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n \).
   - The hyperplane \( H \) is orthogonal to the span of \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n \), so \( H \) divides the space into two half-spaces.
   - Since \( b \) is not a linear combination of \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n \), it must lie on one of the half-spaces determined by \( H \).
   - The other half-space must contain all the vectors \( a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n \), and thus the entire cone \( C \).

3. **Existence of \( y \)**:
