<start_of_turn>model
","To solve this problem, we use the time dilation formula:

\[
t_{\text{clock in Earth}} = \frac{t_{\text{clock in spaceship}}}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^2}}
\]

Where:
- \( t_{\text{clock in Earth}} \) is the time measured by the Earth clock,
- \( t_{\text{clock in spaceship}} \) is the time measured by the spaceship clock,
- \( v \) is the speed of the spaceship relative to Earth,
- \( c \) is the speed of light.

Given:
- \( t_{\text{clock in spaceship}} = 3 \text{ years} \) (clock in the cockpit)
- \( v = 0.6c \)

Substitute the values into the formula:

\[
t_{\text{clock in Earth}'} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6)^2}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]

Thus, the time shown on the clock on Earth is \(\boxed{3.75 \text{ years}}\).
