","To calculate the normal gravity \(g\) at point P, we use the first-order approximation formula:

\[
g = g_0 \left(1 + \frac{g_0}{g_0 - g_0 \cos^2 \theta}\right)
\]

where:
- \(g_0 = 9806.648 \, \text{m/s}^2\) (gravitational acceleration at the equator),
- \(\theta = 60^\circ\) (latitude),
- \(g_0 = 9806.648 \times \left(\frac{a}{r}\right)^2\) (gravitational acceleration at the point P),
- \(a = 6358.388 \, \text{m}\) (mean equatorial radius),
- \(r = 6362.121 \, \text{km} = 6362.121 \times 10^3 \, \text{m}\) (distance from the point P to the centre).

First, calculate the mean equatorial radius \(a\) using the ratio between the polar and equatorial semi-axes:
\[
a = \frac{a_p}{0.9966} = \frac{6358.388}{0.9966} = 6370.382 \, \text{m}
\]

Next, compute \(g_0\):
\[
g_0 = 9806.648 \times \left(\frac{6370.382}{6362.121 \times 10^3}\right)^2 = 9806.648 \times \left(\frac{6370.382}{6362.121}\right)^2 = 9806.648 \times 1.00012 = 9807.628 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

Now, compute \(g\):
\[
g = 9807.628 \left(1 + \frac{9807.628}{9807.628 - 9807.628 \cos^2 60^\circ}\right) = 9807.628 \left(1 + \frac{9807.628}{9807.628 - 9807.628 \times 0.25}\right) = 9807.628 \times 1.00012 = 9807.628 \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

Convert \(g\) to mGal:
\[
g = 9807.628 \, \text{m/s}^2 \times 9.81 \, \text{mGal/s}^2 = 98,076.28 \, \text{mGal}
\]

Thus, the value of normal gravity at point P is \(\boxed{98,076.28 \, \text{mGal}}\).
