A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The contribution to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy is calculated using the formula for the Hall-Oenswald rule:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{G}{2} \left( \frac{b}{d} \right) \left( \frac{1}{1 + \frac{d}{d_0}} \right)
\]  
where:  
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\) (shear modulus),  
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m (Burgers vector),  
- \(d = 10^{-8}\) m (particle diameter),  
- \(d_0 = 6 \times 10^{-8}\) m (average center-to-center spacing).  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9}{2} \left( \frac{0.286 \times 10^{-9}}{10^{-8}} \right) \left( \frac{1}{1 + \frac{10^{-8}}{6 \times 10^{-8}}} \right)
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9}{2} \left( 0.0286 \right) \left( \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{6}} \right)
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9}{2} \left( 0.0286 \right) \left( \frac{6}{7} \right)
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 26 \times 10^9 \times 0.0286 \times 0.844
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 6.5 \times 10^8 \text{ N m}^{-2}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 650 \text{ MPa}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{650 \text{ MPa}}\).
