","To determine the reaction times that can be followed, we need to calculate the residence time of the solution in the flow tube. The residence time is the average time for a drop of solution to travel from the mixing chamber to the observation point.

1. Calculate the cross-sectional area of the tubing:
Diameter = 0.1 mm, so radius = 0.05 mm = 50 μm
Area (A) = πr² = π(50 × 10⁻⁶ m)² ≈ 7.85 × 10⁻8 m²

2. Calculate the flow velocity:
Flow rate (Q) = 10 cm³ min⁻¹ = 10 × 10⁻6 m³ min⁻¹
Convert to m³/s: 10 × 10⁻⁶ m³ min⁻¹ × (1 min/60 s) = 1.67 × 10⁻⁷ m³/s
Velocity (v) = Q / A = (1.67 × 10⁻⁷ m³/s) / (7.85 × 10⁻⁸ m²) ≈ 0.219 m/s

3. Calculate the residence time for different tube lengths:
For a tube length (L) of 1 cm (0.1 m):
Time (t) = L / v = 0.1 m / 0.219 m/s ≈ 0.46 s

For a tube length of 10 cm (1 m):
Time (t) = L / v = 1 m / 0.219 m/s ≈ 4.6 s

So, the reaction times that can be followed with a flow tube 1–10 cm downstream from the mixing chamber are approximately 0.46 s to 4.6 s.
